'''Oita Prefecture''' ({{lang|ja|大分県}}, ''Oita-ken'') is an archipelago [[Prefectures of Okaiken|prefecture]] of [[Okaiken]] located in the [[Nankai]] Region in the Shendan Ocean, approximately 500 km southeast of the Yagikoshu mainland. With a population of 471,384 as of 2026, it is Okaiken's most remote prefecture—a scattered chain of volcanic islands centred on the large island of Oita, whose central volcano of [[Mount Oita]] ({{nihongo|大分岳|Oita-dake}}, 1,128 m) dominates the archipelago.

Oita Prefecture is defined by its isolation and its maritime economy. The prefecture is Okaiken's primary centre for small-ship building and maritime logistics in the southeastern Shendan Ocean, and the [[Port of Oita]] serves as a critical resupply hub for the deep-water fishing fleets and shipping lanes that connect Yagikoshu to the Shendan Ocean trade routes.

== History ==

=== The island shipbuilders ===
Oita's shipbuilding tradition is as old as the islands themselves. The volcanic islands, scattered across hundreds of kilometres of open ocean, required seaworthy vessels for inter-island transport, fishing, and trade, and the Oita shipwrights developed a distinctive tradition of wooden boat-building that is still practised today.

The {{nihongo|Oita Sengokubune|大分船|Oita-bune}}—a sturdy, double-ended sailing vessel with a high bow and stern, designed for the heavy seas of the southeastern Shendan—was the primary trading vessel of the region from the 14th to the 19th century. The Oita shipyards produced approximately 200 vessels per year at their peak, supplying the fishing and trading fleets of the entire southern Shendan basin.

The transition from wooden sailing vessels to steel motorised ships in the 20th century nearly destroyed the traditional shipbuilding industry, but the Oita shipyards adapted by specialising in small-to-medium vessels—fishing boats, coasters, ferry craft, and patrol vessels—that are too small for the major shipyards of [[Seto]] and [[Ishijima]] but too specialised for mass production.

=== The volcanic islands ===
[[File:Mount Oita Volcano.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Mount Oita, an active volcano dominating the main island of the archipelago]]
Mount Oita is an active volcano whose eruptions have shaped the archipelago's geography and history. The {{nihongo|Great Oita Eruption|大分大噴火|Oita Dai-funka}} of 1783 produced a massive ash fall and pyroclastic flow that destroyed three villages on the island's southern coast and created the {{nihongo|Oita Lava Field|大分溶岩原|Oita Yōgan-bara}}, a barren expanse of volcanic rock that still covers approximately 15 km² of the island. The volcano's last significant eruption occurred in 1967, when a Strombolian episode produced lava flows that reached the sea, extending the island's coastline by approximately 200 metres.

== Geography ==

Oita Prefecture is an archipelago comprising the main island of Oita (approximately 587 km²) and 11 smaller islands scattered across a 400 km arc in the southeastern Shendan Ocean. The main island is roughly triangular, with the volcanic peak of Mount Oita at its centre and the capital city of [[Oita]] on the northern coast.

The smaller islands range from the substantial [[Miyajima]] (34 km²) and [[Funaya]] (288 km²) to the tiny [[Kojima]] (23 km²) and [[Awashima]] (13 km²). Most of the smaller islands are volcanic in origin, with rugged coastlines and limited flat land for agriculture.

=== Climate ===
Oita Prefecture has a [[w:Tropical monsoon climate|tropical monsoon climate]] (''Am''), with warm, humid conditions year-round. Summer temperatures consistently exceed 30°C, and winter temperatures rarely drop below 16°C. The islands receive significant rainfall, particularly during the typhoon season (November–April), and the archipelago's exposed position in the southeastern Shendan makes it one of the most typhoon-affected areas in Okaiken.

